semgrep==1.178.0

# Security override - minimum version to close a CVE that this spec's resolver
# would otherwise be free to peg to a vulnerable release.
# cryptography: GHSA-g6cj-pr64-35w5 / CVE-2026-69247 (PKCS#7 EnvelopedData
# decryption leaks a Bleichenbacher oracle through distinguishable errors and
# timing; affects >=44.0.0,<50.0.0). Arrives here transitively via pyjwt[crypto].
# The vulnerable path is unreachable from SESTRAV - nothing in tracked source
# imports cryptography and there are no PKCS#7 / EnvelopedData / S-MIME call
# sites - so this is defence in depth. It is pinned rather than dismissed
# because a real upstream fix exists, and floored rather than left to resolve
# to latest so a future recompile cannot silently walk it back.
# EXIT CONDITION: drop this floor once semgrep's own dependency graph requires
# cryptography>=50.0.1 without help, then recompile and re-verify hashes.
cryptography>=50.0.1

# pyjwt has NO floor here, because a floor cannot reach a patched release:
# semgrep 1.177.0 (pinned above) and 1.178.0 (the latest on 2026-09-30) both
# declare pyjwt[crypto]~=2.13.0, so a >=2.14.0 floor makes this spec unsatisfiable
# (ResolutionImpossible). A hand-edited pin in the .txt failed the same way at
# install time, taking down both semgrep jobs in security.yml. pyjwt is instead
# lifted by the uv override file environments/semgrep-overrides.txt, which
# tools/update_dependencies.py passes for this spec. That file records the scan
# equivalence measured before adopting it, why the lock must now be installed
# with --no-deps, and the exit condition.
